\subsection{Does a win come from the design, the estimator, or both?}
\label{sec:res-ablation}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{../figures/fig4_ablation}
\caption{RMSE relative to uniform~$\times$~mean at $n=100$ (geometric mean over non-thin benchmarks) for each design (rows) and estimator (columns); colour is log-scaled and centred at 1 (blue better than uniform, red worse). Boxed italic entries: the 95\% bootstrap interval includes 1. Blank: not run. $\dagger$ IRT-CAT chooses items per target, a different protocol, so its cells are not paired with the shared-subset designs.}
\label{fig:ablation}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:ablation} crosses six designs with the estimators.
Within each estimator column the designs that keep every item drawable and spread the sample over difficulty (stratified, PPS, balanced) are best and close to one another, and information-top selection is among the worst in every column, so a gain attributed to ``smart'' item selection depends on the estimator and on the selection rule.
On uniform design the difference estimator is better than the model-only IRT estimator by a factor of two in extrapolation ($0.85$ against $1.96$) and by about $10\%$ in interpolation ($0.70$ against $0.78$).
With the mean estimator, balanced selection improves on stratified sampling ($0.73$ against $0.81$ in interpolation, $0.81$ against $0.85$ in extrapolation, $0.77$ against $0.83$ in lineage), and with the difference estimator it improves on uniform design by $3$--$5$ points of the ratio ($0.65$ against $0.70$, $0.82$ against $0.85$, $0.70$ against $0.73$).
The adaptive IRT-CAT design is competitive in interpolation and lineage ($0.68$ and $0.67$ with the difference estimator) and not in extrapolation ($1.04$ $[0.91,1.10]$).

